==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*leudaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*lewdʰ-||to grow, come up}}.  Cognates include Old Saxon {{term/t|osx|liodan}}, Old High German {{term/t|goh|liotan}}, Gothic {{term/t|got|𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽}}.  The Indo-European root is also the source of Albanian {{term|lind||to be born, to spring|lang=sq}} < {{term|lej||to generate, produce, give birth to|lang=sq}}, Ancient Greek {{term/t|grc|ἐλεύθερος||free, freeman}}, Latin {{term/t|la|liber||free}}, Old Irish {{term/t|sga|luss||plant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈleːodan/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|head=lēodan}}

# to [[grow]]
#: ''Of ðam twige '''ludon''' reðe wæstme'': from that branch sprang dire fruits.  (Cædmon's ''Metrical Paraphrase'')

